Agent Response: Good — I can help you get organized. Below are targeted questions to ask Amy Foster and a practical evidence checklist and next-step plan so you’re ready for the next legal action/hearing. These are written so you can copy them into an email or ask them in your meeting.

Questions to ask your attorney (Amy Foster, Caldwell Legal)
- Case strategy & urgency
  - How strong is our current case based on the discovery materials we gathered?
  - Do you think we need more evidence before the hearing, or is the existing record sufficient?
  - What is the likely hearing timeline in San Diego County (civil division) and how flexible is that date?
  - If we need more time to gather evidence, what’s the process and likelihood of getting a continuance?

- Specific evidence needs (financial)
  - Which specific financial documents will be most persuasive for my immigration status review (bank statements, tax returns, pay stubs, loan documents, transfer records, ledgers, property titles)?
  - Do we need certified copies (e.g., certified tax transcripts) or originals for court?
  - Should we get a forensic accountant or another expert to analyze finances (e.g., to show support, sponsor ability, hidden transfers)?

- Specific evidence needs (custody / family)
  - What custody-related evidence will the court consider most relevant (custody orders, parenting plans, school records, medical records, daycare records, text/email messages about custody, supervised-visit logs)?
  - Do we need declarations from caregivers, teachers, or social workers? How should they be written (content, format, notarization)?
  - Are expert custody/psych evaluations likely to help? If so, when should we retain them?

- Witnesses & statements
  - Which witnesses should we subpoena or ask for written declarations?
  - How do we preserve witness availability and credibility? Do we need signed, dated declarations under penalty of perjury?
  - When and how should we exchange witness lists and exhibits with the respondent?

- Admissibility & evidentiary issues
  - What types of evidence will be excluded as hearsay or otherwise inadmissible in this proceeding, and how can we make sure key items are admissible (authentication, business records affidavit, stipulations)?
  - Are there foundation or chain-of-custody steps we must take for electronic evidence (text messages, social media, CCTV, voicemail)?

- Discovery follow-ups & remedies
  - Are there any unanswered or incomplete discovery responses from the respondent we should move to compel before the hearing?
  - If the respondent produced documents late or incompletely, can we request sanctions or an evidentiary inference?

- Motions & pre-hearing submissions
  - Should we file any pre-hearing motions (motions in limine, motions to compel, motions to exclude evidence)?
  - What deadlines apply for filing exhibit lists, witness lists, proposed findings, or pre-hearing briefs in this division?

- Logistics & costs
  - Estimated timeline and cost for obtaining additional evidence and expert opinions.
  - Best format and file size for sharing documents with you (PDF, scanned, Bates-stamped, secure upload portal?).
  - Do you recommend mediation or settlement discussions before the hearing?

Document checklist to gather ASAP (prioritized)
1. High priority (get these first)
   - Recent bank statements (last 2–3 years) and cleared checks.
   - Tax returns (federal & state) for last 3 years, W-2s, 1099s.
   - Pay stubs and proof of income.
   - Any court orders, custody orders, temporary orders.
   - Communications (text chains, WhatsApp, emails) relating to custody, financial support, or immigration/status issues — exported and saved with timestamps.
   - Identification/immigration records (passports, visas, I-94s, prior filings).

2. Medium priority
   - School records, medical records, vaccination records, daycare attendance records for children.
   - Lease/mortgage docs, utility bills proving residence.
   - Transaction histories that show transfers, gifts, or debts between parties.
   - Any police reports, incident reports, or CPS correspondence if relevant.

3. Lower priority (but useful)
   - Social media posts/screenshots that corroborate timeline or conduct (save meta-data where possible).
   - Photographs, video, or CCTV footage with date/time stamps.
   - Declarations from friends/family/employers with first-hand knowledge.

How to prepare documents for your attorney
- Scan to PDF, name files clearly (e.g., “Bank_BofA_2019-2025_Account_1234.pdf”).
- Create a one-page index/exhibit list with short descriptions and dates.
- Keep originals safe; provide certified copies when requested.
- Get signed declarations under penalty of perjury for key witnesses; ask Amy for a suggested template.

Questions about experts & costs
- Would an expert (forensic accountant, custody evaluator, psychologist) materially change our position?
- If yes, who do you recommend and what’s the estimated timeline and cost?
- Do we need to serve expert reports pre-hearing? Are there disclosure deadlines?

Pre-hearing strategy
- What are the non-negotiable pieces of evidence we must have at hearing (e.g., custody order, major financial proof)?
- Which issues can we concede or narrow to reduce the scope of the hearing?
- How should witnesses be prepared/testified (direct exam outline, exhibits ready)?

Contingencies & settlement
- If the respondent offers a settlement, what benchmarks should we demand to protect immigration interests and custody/financial outcomes?
- If the hearing is scheduled soon and we can’t obtain everything, what’s the minimum solid record you recommend bringing?

Practical checklist for you this week
- Gather/high-priority documents listed above.
- Export and back up any relevant messages (texts, emails) preserving timestamps.
- Make a list of potential witnesses with contact info and short statement of what each would say.
- Decide whether you want expert evaluation now or to wait for Amy’s recommendation.

Next steps I can take for you
- Draft a concise email to Amy listing these specific questions and attaching your prioritized documents.
- Prepare a one-page exhibit index and a folder structure for her review.
- If you’d like, I can draft suggested witness declaration templates or a sample subpoena request for your attorney to review.

Quick confirmation
- Would you like me to draft an email to Amy Foster with these questions and an attached document checklist? If so, tell me:
  1) do you want the email sent now or saved as a draft?
  2) do you want me to include anything else (tone, summary of facts, attachments)?
- Also: I have a note saved that you asked legal-related messages be sent to vince2000@gmail.com in past conversations. Do you want me to also forward this list to that address or keep it only between you and Amy?

